Git Command Cheat Sheet
🔧 Setup
git --version
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "you@example.com"
git config --list

📁 Start a Repository
git init
git clone <repository-url>

📊 Check Status
git status
git log
git log --oneline
git diff

➕ Add Files
git add filename.py
git add .
git add -A

💾 Commit
git commit -m "Your commit message"
git commit -am "Your commit message"

🌐 Remote Repository
git remote -v
git remote add origin <repository-url>
git remote remove origin

⬆️ Push
git push
git push -u origin main


After setting upstream once:

git push

⬇️ Pull
git pull
git pull origin main

🌿 Branches
git branch
git branch -a
git branch new-branch
git switch new-branch
git switch -c new-branch
git switch main
git branch -d new-branch


Older equivalent:

git checkout new-branch
git checkout -b new-branch

🔀 Merge
git switch main
git merge new-branch

❌ Undo Changes

Discard changes in a file:

git restore filename.py


Unstage a file:

git restore --staged filename.py


Undo the latest commit but keep changes:

git reset --soft HEAD~1


Undo the latest commit and discard changes:

git reset --hard HEAD~1


⚠️ Be careful with --hard because it can permanently remove uncommitted work.

🏷️ Tags
git tag
git tag v1.0.0
git push origin v1.0.0

🧹 Useful Cleanup
git branch -d branch-name
git fetch --prune
git clean -n


git clean -n shows what would be deleted without actually deleting it.

🔍 Find Things
git log --oneline --all
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
git show <commit-id>
git blame filename.py

🚀 Typical Git Workflow

For everyday development, you'll often use:

git status
git add .
git commit -m "Add new feature"
git push


When starting work:

git pull
git switch -c feature-name


When finished:

git add .
git commit -m "Complete feature"
git push -u origin feature-name


Most important commands to remember: git status → git add → git commit → git push → git pull → git switch.